  "answer": "<p>The best approach among these options is the <b>secondary ELB with Route53 Weighted Round Robin traffic distribution</b>. Using this setup, you can methodically control the percentage of users routed to the updated application stack with fine precision, down to one percent increments, thanks to the weighted records. Rollback is rapid and simple—just switch the DNS weights to direct traffic back to the original environment. This meets all the listed requirements: gradual rollout, precise user percentage control, and quick recovery.</p><p><i>'Use the Blue-Green deployment pattern'</i> (Blue-Green) lets you cut over all users at once to new instances, but this approach cannot adjust traffic proportionally over time; it’s an all-or-nothing switchover, so it fails the requirement of granular traffic control.</p><p><i>'Replace your existing Launch Configuration’s AMI'</i> (AMIs in Launch Config) and scaling old/new instances allows for gradual replacement but lacks the ability to precisely control what percentage of users see which version at a given time, given that ELB load balancing is instance-based, not user-percentage or version-based.</p><p><i>'Switch to AWS Elastic Beanstalk'</i> provides convenient deployment mechanisms (like Rolling Deployments and rollbacks), but these don’t enable you to adjust user exposure to the new version at a granular, percentage-based level during a rollout.</p><p><b>Tip:</b> To control user exposure down to the percentage level in AWS, leveraging multiple ELBs with Route53 weighted DNS records is a common and reliable solution.</p>",
  "query": "<p>You plan to roll out a new version of your application to production, but due to its high risk, you want to carefully control the release over several hours. Your setup uses EC2 instances in Auto Scaling Groups behind an Elastic Load Balancer, with custom AMIs assigned to your Launch Configurations. Crucially, you need to control exactly what percent of users receive the new version, and you want to be able to roll back rapidly if any issues arise. Also, there are no changes required at the database level during this deployment. Given these constraints, what is the optimal strategy to safely deploy and, if necessary, efficiently revert the application update?</p>",
